.TH CONFFILE 5 "October 2026" "apkg 0.3.4" "Amix File Formats"
.SH NAME
conffile \- package configuration files that keep your edits across upgrades
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.I conffile
is a configuration file that a package ships with a default and that the
administrator edits in place, such as
.B /etc/apkg.conf
or a daemon's own configuration.  A package marks it as an editable
object of class
.B conffile
in its pkgmap(5)
.RB ( "e conffile /etc/rsyncd.conf" ),
and carries the two class action scripts
.B i.conffile
and
.BR r.conffile .
They run under every
.BR pkgadd (1M)
and
.BR pkgrm (1M),
the stock ones included, and under
.BR apkg (1).
.PP
Most of the system's own settings do not need this: a package ships its
defaults in a file it owns, and your changes go in a separate file that
no package owns (as module parameters do in
.BR modreg.local.d ).
Conffiles are for files that have to be edited where they are.
.SS Installing and upgrading
For each conffile,
.B i.conffile
compares three things: the file on disk, the new default in the package,
and the
.I baseline
\- a copy of the default the package shipped last time, kept in
.BI /var/sadm/conffiles/ pkginst / path\fR.
.TP 4
\(bu
No file and no baseline (first install): the default is installed.
.TP
\(bu
The file equals the new default, or still equals the old baseline (you
never edited it): the new default is installed, silently.
.TP
\(bu
The file differs from both (you edited it, or something other than the
package wrote it): your file is kept.  The new default is written beside
it as
.IB file .new\fR,
the file is listed in
.BR /var/sadm/conffiles/PENDING ,
and pkgadd prints
.RS
.IB file ": kept your version; the new default is " file .new
.RE
.IP
If the package's default has not changed since the last install, an
edited file is simply kept, with no message and no new
.B .new
file.
.TP
\(bu
The file is gone but its baseline exists (you removed it): the package
puts its default back and says so.  The package tools require every file
a package owns to exist after the install, so a removed conffile cannot
stay removed.
.PP
In every case the new default becomes the baseline.
.SS What to do with a .new file
Compare the two, for example with
.BR diff (1):
.RS
.B diff /etc/apkg.conf /etc/apkg.conf.new
.RE
.PP
Then either keep your file and delete the
.B .new
file, or move the
.B .new
file over yours (and carry your changes into it).  The next install or
upgrade of the package notices either choice: a file equal to the new
default leaves
.B PENDING
and loses its stale
.BR .new .
.B apkg
lists the pending files after each transaction.
.PP
A plain
.BR pkgchk (1M)
reports a conffile you replaced by hand until the package database is
told about it, with
.B installf \-c conffile
.I pkginst path
.B e
and then
.B installf \-f
.IR pkginst ;
.B pkgchk \-n
skips editable files and is always the clean post-install check.
.SS Removing
.B r.conffile
removes a conffile you never edited, together with its baseline.  A file
you edited is kept, with its baseline, and pkgrm says so; it then belongs
to no package.  Any
.B .new
file and its
.B PENDING
line are removed in both cases.  Installing the package again finds the
kept file and its baseline, and the rules above apply.
.SH FILES
.PD 0
.TP 36
.BI /var/sadm/conffiles/ pkginst / path
the baseline: the default the package last shipped for
.I path
.TP
.B /var/sadm/conffiles/PENDING
one
.RI \(lq "pkginst path" \(rq
line per conffile whose new default waits in
.IB path .new
.TP
.IB path .new
the new default beside a kept file
.PD
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apkg (1),
.BR pkgadd (1M),
.BR pkgrm (1M),
.BR pkgchk (1M),
.BR installf (1M),
.BR apkg.conf (5),
.BR admin (5),
.BR pkgmap (5)
.SH NOTES
The scripts use only Bourne
.BR sh (1)
and core commands
.RB ( cp ,
.BR cmp ,
.BR mkdir ,
.BR rm ,
.BR rmdir ,
.BR mv ,
.BR expr ),
so a package keeps your file under any installer.  One conffile has one
owning package.
